{"type":"text","timestamp":1791654145397,"sessionID":"ses_76297109ac90d682289b","part":{"id":"prt_126e8ad61001dY5b1TUxj5r2EI","sessionID":"ses_76297109ac90d682289b","messageID":"msg_126e8992b00167jPaKWOi0wVyx","type":"text","text":"I've converted `customers.csv` into `customers.json`, an array of 87 records in the original row order.\n\n- **`id`:** kept as a string, so leading zeros stay (`\"00000\"` through `\"00086\"`).\n- **`note`:** kept as a string. Embedded newlines (`\"two\\nlines\"`), escaped quotes (`a \"quote\"`), commas and accented characters (`olá, mundo`) all come through intact. Notes that were empty in the CSV are empty strings (`\"\"`).\n- **`active`:** turned into `true` or `false` (29 are `true`). The script would have stopped with an error on any other value, and none turned up.\n- **`age`:** blank became `null` (22 rows); everything else became an integer.\n\nThe file ends each row with a Windows-style line break, while the line breaks inside notes are plain newlines. The script used a proper CSV parser, so the multi-line notes weren't split into extra rows. Afterwards I loaded the JSON back in: the ids run in sequence with no gaps, so no rows were lost or reordered.\n\nThe conversion was a one-off script, so I didn't add it to the repo. Only `customers.json` was created.","time":{"start":1791654145377,"end":1791654145397}}}
